# group syncables: the three writes, their registrations and the dispatch they share.
#
# Run: 15 run, 12 caught, 3 survived, 0 harness faults. Two of the survivors are the controls.
#
# The third, `link_methods_swapped_post_for_delete`, is a **known and unfixable** survivor on an
# unlicensed stack, and it is kept deliberately rather than deleted. `link_group_syncable` and
# `unlink_group_syncable` both stop at the same `requireLicense` 501, so swapping which one is
# wired to POST and which to DELETE produces byte-identical responses and no test can see it.
# The two diverge only behind the licence, where `link` answers 201 with the marshalled syncable
# and `unlink` answers 200 with `{"status":"OK"}` — so this line becomes catchable exactly when
# [D-390] is closed, and is the cheapest check that it really was.
